WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.752
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
How do you recover

00:00:01.752 --> 00:00:03.970
from an unexpected
subscriber problem?

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:05.790
What happens to the messages?

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.110
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.200
Cat-Out, our asynchronous
order processing service,

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.140
is now looking to deploy
an updated subscriber

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.770
code, which is great.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.220
But the challenge is
the new executable

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.590
may erroneously
acknowledge messages,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.210
leading to message loss.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.940
Best way to deal with this is--

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.080
well, you guessed it.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.470
Don't deploy the new code.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:34.950
Well, is that ever an option?

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.580
That's mostly not an option.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.460
So what should they really do?

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.400
Well, there is a feature
in Cloud Pub/Sub called

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.580
Seek which extends the
subscriber functionality

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.160
by allowing you to change
the acknowledgment state

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.990
of messages in bulk.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:50.820
This means you can
replay previously

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.410
acknowledged messages.

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.050
There are two ways to
perform seek operations.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:58.680
You can seek to a snapshot
or to a specific time.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:01.050
If you seek to a
snapshot, it allows

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.940
you to capture the
message acknowledgment

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.540
state of the subscription.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.050
This means once a
snapshot is created,

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.080
it retains all messages
that were unacknowledged

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.810
in the source subscription
at the time of the snapshot's

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.960
creation and any other
messages published to the topic

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.100
after that.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.500
You can replay these
unacknowledged messages

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.990
by using a snapshot
to seek to any

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.700
of the topic's subscriptions.

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.070
Snapshots expire,
and are deleted

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:29.070
if the oldest unacknowledged
message in the snapshot

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.500
exceeds the message
retention duration.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.260
So if your subscription
was set to retain messages

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.590
for, say, seven days,
but you were a day

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.630
behind in processing
the messages,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:41.520
then the snapshot
of the subscription

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.430
would expire after six days.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.310
You can use the same snapshot
for multiple subscriptions,

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:49.260
as long as they
have the same topic.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.560
Seeking to a time marks every
message received by Pub/Sub up

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.070
before that time
is acknowledged,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.040
and all the messages
received after that time

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.120
as unacknowledged.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.400
But to use the feature,
you have to choose

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.830
to retain acknowledged
messages on the subscription.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:07.920
This can be
convenient, but usually

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.960
costs more than snapshots.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.260
It may also be inaccurate,
due to possible clock skews

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.950
amongst that Pub/Sub servers.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.850
So if you want to replay
and reprocess previously

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.030
acknowledged messages,
just seek to a prior time.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:24.040
If Cat-Out is deploying
new code for a subscriber,

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.910
then should they seek
to a snapshot or time?

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.740
Well, they should
seek a snapshot,

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.100
because incorporating snapshots
into the deployment process

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.660
gives a way to recover
from bugs in the code.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.960
If Cat-Out faced an
unexpected subscriber

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.990
problem and no relevant
snapshot is available,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.430
then seeking to past times
is a good way to recover it.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.960
With Cat-Out, we
saw two use cases

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.500
where you can use seek feature
in a new code deployment

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.900
and in an unexpected
subscriber problem.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.320
Seek to snapshots is also useful
when testing subscriber code

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:57.540
for performance and
consistency, because you

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.390
have to use the same
data in every run.

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.510
Snapshots enable consistent
data with strong semantics.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:05.160
That was replaying messages.

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.390
What about discarding?

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.600
What if Cat-Out wants to
just save on processing time

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.380
and cost on backlogged messages
that are no longer relevant?

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:17.340
In situations like that, you can
port or discard those messages.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.800
Just seek to a
time in the future,

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.070
and all those messages will
be considered acknowledged.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:23.650
All right.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.730
So today, we learned how to
replay and purge messages

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.490
in Cloud Pub/Sub
using the Seek feature

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.150
by seeking to a specific point
in time or to a snapshot.

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.503
If you want to try out
the Seek feature yourself,

00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:37.920
check out the quick start
linked in the description below.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.160
Join us next time to learn
more about ordering messages

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:42.540
in Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.273
Until then, give us
a like, subscribe,

00:03:44.273 --> 00:03:45.690
and let us know
what else you want

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.110
to learn about Cloud Pub/Sub.

